zeo answers this differently ON PURPOSE, so `.expected` records ZEO's output
and this file records why -- with ruby's own answer below it.

A class written in a `class << self` body belongs to the SINGLETON class in
real Ruby. Zeo hands the definition to the enclosing module instead, which
keeps the property the code depends on -- the singleton methods beside it
reach it by bare name, as `paint` does -- and pays for it by also answering
the qualified lookup. So the last three lines diverge: zeo answers
`false` / `[:Visitor]` / `"<blue>"`; ruby puts the class on the singleton
class and answers `true` / `[]` / raises. See `docs/COMPATIBILITY.md`
("A class written in a `class << self` body").

--- ruby 4.0.6 answers ---
<red>
String
"String"
true
[]
(then dies: singleton_body_class_and_self_path.rb:45:in '<main>':
uninitialized constant Color::Visitor (NameError), exit 1)
